WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.459
What if your software engineers stopped writing most of the code themselves?

2
00:00:07.768 --> 00:00:09.573
Software was never just code.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.953
Planning, testing, reviewing, shipping.

4
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.286
Each step adds time, turning a simple idea into a twenty-day cycle.

5
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.100
The bottleneck wasn't effort.

6
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.238
It was the process.

7
00:00:20.648 --> 00:00:22.600
So Coinbase changed the workflow.

8
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.589
More than twenty-four hundred developers adopted Cursor,

9
00:00:25.784 --> 00:00:29.295
directing agents through implementation, testing, and review.

10
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.344
The engineer moved up a level: define intent, validate results.

11
00:00:35.945 --> 00:00:40.020
In Coinbase's reported workflow, agents created three out of every four pull

12
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.370
requests. Not a side experiment.

13
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.404
The new default.

14
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.072
Some teams cut the time from idea to production from twenty days

15
00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:53.105
to less than two.

16
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.831
A ninety percent reduction.

17
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.695
Not by typing faster, by removing handoffs.

18
00:01:02.768 --> 00:01:04.789
The results weren't just about speed.

19
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.822
Engineers merged fifty-five percent more pull requests,

20
00:01:08.006 --> 00:01:09.838
and saved seven hours every week.

21
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.920
Small teams started shipping what once took a full team.

22
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.323
The biggest shift may not be how fast we write code,

23
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.055
but how well we direct and review what AI builds.

24
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.011
The next era is already here.
